  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Colour Returns",
    "overview": "Personal data is traded, and the person it describes carries the cost. Koverr shows that in black and white: every leak, every breach, every source of exposure is a grey. Colour is what a person gets back. A leak that has been found, requested, answered and checked turns green, and nothing else on the page is allowed to.\n\nThe page is a near-black market floor with one bone-coloured table and one full green field at the close. Data is the interface: a treemap of real breach records, greyscale photographs of an exchange floor, a dated leak record that changes colour as it is closed. There is no illustration. The look is calm and exact, closer to a ledger than a security dashboard, because the reader arrives worried and needs to feel that someone is keeping the record.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Greyscale for exposure, a single green for fixed and for the main action.",
      "Heavy, compact display type over quiet, generous body type.",
      "Flat fills separated by tone and space. No shadows, no gradients, no borders doing the work of spacing.",
      "Real, sourced figures or clearly labelled fictional ones.",
      "One authored motion: colour returning to a record as it is fixed."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Colour Returns Rule",
        "body": "A leak is grey until it is fixed. No hue marks risk, loss or alarm. Colour is only ever the answer.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Green Rule",
        "body": "There is one chromatic colour. It appears on a fixed item or on the action that fixes it, and on the closing field. If something green is not a fix or an action, remove it.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Shade Not Hue Rule",
        "body": "Differences in exposure are shown by lightness, never by colour. Whoever relies on colour alone loses the meaning, so every shade also has a label.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Figures Rule",
        "body": "Counts, dates and amounts use tabular numerals so columns and tickers line up.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Weight Does The Work Rule",
        "body": "Hierarchy comes from weight and size. Do not add a second typeface, uppercase eyebrows or tracking tricks.",
        "section": "typography"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** show exposure in grey and fixes in green (#4be38a), and let the only colour on a record be the part that has been dealt with.",
      "**Do** separate surfaces with tone and 3px gaps.",
      "**Do** pair every shade with a label or a figure.",
      "**Do** keep copy honest: sourced figures or labelled fictional data, never guaranteed removals.",
      "**Do** keep motion to colour returning, and give it a reduced-motion state that shows the finished result."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use red, coral or amber for risk or loss. Risk is grey.",
      "**Don't** use green for anything that is not a fix or the action to fix.",
      "**Don't** add illustration, cartoon characters or decorative graphics.",
      "**Don't** add shadows, gradients, glass or border-left accent stripes.",
      "**Don't** stage sections with scroll fades, or animate tiles in.",
      "**Don't** set type under 14px or introduce a second typeface without a role Archivo cannot perform."
    ]
  }
}